<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Electron app entitlements. Union of what Electron's own hardened
       runtime needs and what src-tauri/Entitlements.plist already granted the
       Tauri build. This is `mac.entitlements` (the APP's own entitlements,
       read by codesign's outer/final pass) — NOT
       sidecar/sidecar-entitlements.plist, which stays a separate file signed
       into only the sidecar executable by scripts/afterSign.mjs. Giving the
       app process the sidecar's grants (disable-library-validation,
       allow-unsigned-executable-memory, allow-dyld-environment-variables)
       would be free-riding privilege the app itself does not need.

       GOTCHA, found empirically running this file through a real codesign:
       `plutil -lint` accepts a bare double hyphen inside an XML comment
       body, but the kernel-level AMFI entitlements deserializer codesign
       actually hands this file to (AMFIUnserializeXML) does not — it is
       strict XML, which forbids two adjacent hyphen characters anywhere in
       a comment except at the delimiters. A prose comment written with a
       double hyphen (a common dash habit) parses fine under plutil and then
       fails signing with an opaque "syntax error near line N". Every
       comment in this file uses an em dash instead, deliberately, for
       exactly that reason (this paragraph included, on its second pass). -->

  <!-- Mandatory for V8/JIT under the hardened runtime. Electron aborts on
       launch without it (Electron docs, "Signing and notarizing macOS
       builds"); electron-builder's own default entitlements template
       (app-builder-lib/templates/entitlements.mac.plist) carries the same
       key, this just makes it explicit alongside the app-specific grants
       below rather than splitting the truth across two files. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- src-tauri/Entitlements.plist, verbatim intent: dictation/voice notes/
       meeting recording read the mic through the renderer's getUserMedia.
       hardenedRuntime BLOCKS mic access without this entitlement even with
       NSMicrophoneUsageDescription present and the user's Privacy toggle on. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Required if (and only if) mac.identity resolves to "-" (ad-hoc). Ad-hoc
       signing plus hardenedRuntime:true (electron-builder's default) means
       library validation rejects Electron's own pre-signed frameworks, which
       carry a DIFFERENT (Electron's) Team ID than the ad-hoc "no team" the
       app itself signs under — electron-builder's own MacTargetHelper warns
       about exactly this combination. It ALSO covers foreign-Team-ID dylibs
       under the sidecar tree if this process ever dlopen's one in-process
       (it does not today, but the grant is what makes that safe if it ever
       does). A real Developer ID identity does not need this: the whole
       tree is signed under one real Team ID, so library validation never
       trips. Harmless to carry unconditionally either way — it only
       RELAXES a check, so shipping it on the Developer-ID path is not a
       security regression, just unnecessary. Kept here rather than made
       conditional on identity so this file's shape is signature-stable
       (macsign.sh's guard reads `codesign -dv` output, not this file, so
       there's no correctness reason to vary it). -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- electron-builder's own default entitlements template
       (app-builder-lib/templates/entitlements.mac.plist) grants this to the
       app; the Tauri build never granted it to Arcelle.app itself (only to
       the sidecar, via sidecar-entitlements.plist). Kept here deliberately,
       matching electron-builder's own baseline rather than trimming it —
       V8's JIT allocator can want RWX pages under some configurations, and
       Electron's upstream template treats it as part of the same "let V8
       run" grant as allow-jit above. Revisit only if a build is observed to
       work without it. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
